    body: '<p>A <b>pointer</b> holds the <em>address</em> of another variable. <code>&amp;x</code> means "the address of x", and <code>*p</code> means "the thing p points to". A pointer variable is declared with a star: <code>int *p = &amp;x;</code>.</p><p>Because C copies arguments, a function cannot change your variable directly. Hand it a pointer instead and it can reach back and change the original. That is how <code>scanf</code> works too.</p>',
